---
import "../styles/global.css";

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en-AU">
  <head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="Two table tennis serves arrive at your bat at the same height and speed, spinning opposite ways. The bat angle that returns them differs by more than twenty degrees. A simulation you can drive."
    />
    <title>Spin tells your bat where to point</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <a class="skip" href="#instrument">Skip to the simulation</a>

    <header class="masthead">
      <nav aria-label="Primary">
        <a href={base}>Bat angle</a>
        <a href="#instrument">The shot</a>
        <a href="#map">Every shot</a>
        <a href="#why">Why</a>
      </nav>
    </header>

    <main>
      <section class="hero">
        <p class="eyebrow">Table tennis, from the receiving end</p>
        <h1>Spin tells your bat where to point</h1>
        <p class="standfirst">
          Two serves reach your bat at the same height, within half a metre per second of the
          same speed. One is spinning backwards at about 112 turns a second; the other forwards
          at about 104. Everything a beginner is taught to watch — the speed, the height, the
          bounce — is the same. The bat angle that returns them is more than twenty degrees
          apart, and using the wrong one doesn't make the shot slightly worse. It puts the ball
          in the net.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section id="instrument" class="instrument" aria-labelledby="instrument-heading">
        <h2 id="instrument-heading">Take the shot</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The serve has already bounced on your half and is coming up to your bat. You choose two
          things: how far the face is tilted back, and which way you swing. Everything else — how
          hard, where you stand — is fixed.
        </p>

        <div class="stage">
          <canvas
            id="table-view"
            role="img"
            aria-label="Side view of a table tennis table. Set the controls below to see the return."
          ></canvas>
          <p class="legend">
            <span class="key key-serve">the serve</span>
            <span class="key key-return">your return</span>
            <span class="key key-swing">swing direction</span>
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="controls">
          <fieldset class="serve-picker">
            <legend>The serve coming at you</legend>
            <div class="radios">
              <label>
                <input type="radio" name="serve" value="backspin" checked />
                <span>Backspin</span>
              </label>
              <label>
                <input type="radio" name="serve" value="topspin" />
                <span>Topspin</span>
              </label>
            </div>
          </fieldset>

          <div class="slider">
            <label for="bat-angle">
              Bat angle
              <output id="bat-angle-value" for="bat-angle">0°</output>
              <span id="bat-angle-hint" class="hint">vertical</span>
            </label>
            <input id="bat-angle" type="range" min="-30" max="70" step="1" value="0" />
            <p class="scale"><span>closed, over the ball</span><span>open, face back</span></p>
          </div>

          <div class="slider">
            <label for="swing-direction">
              Swing direction
              <output id="swing-direction-value" for="swing-direction">5°</output>
              <span id="swing-direction-hint" class="hint">brushing up</span>
            </label>
            <input id="swing-direction" type="range" min="-60" max="80" step="1" value="5" />
            <p class="scale"><span>down, chopping</span><span>up, brushing</span></p>
          </div>

          <div class="verdict">
            <p id="outcome-label" class="outcome">NET</p>
            <p id="outcome-detail" class="outcome-detail">It never gets over.</p>
            <button id="find-shot" type="button">Move me to a shot that lands</button>
          </div>
        </div>

        <p id="outcome-live" class="visually-hidden" role="status" aria-live="polite"></p>
      </section>

      <section id="map" class="map" aria-labelledby="map-heading">
        <h2 id="map-heading">Every shot at once</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          Every bat angle against every swing direction, played out and coloured by where the ball
          ended up. Green lands. Now change the serve and watch the green move.
        </p>
        <div class="map-grid">
          <div>
            <canvas
              id="solution-map"
              role="img"
              aria-label="A map of which bat angles and swing directions return the serve."
            ></canvas>
            <p class="axis-label">Bat angle runs bottom to top; swing direction left to right.</p>
          </div>
          <div class="map-side">
            <p class="legend">
              <span class="key key-in">lands</span>
              <span class="key key-net">net</span>
              <span class="key key-out">long</span>
              <span class="key key-ghost">the other serve's answer</span>
            </p>
            <p id="map-summary" class="summary">Working out every shot in range…</p>
            <p id="map-overlap" class="overlap"></p>
            <p class="aside">
              That is the whole point. There is very nearly no setting that covers both serves, so
              reading the spin isn't a refinement you add once your technique is good. It's the
              first thing you have to do, every single ball.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section id="why" class="why" aria-labelledby="why-heading">
        <h2 id="why-heading">Why the angle has to move</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            Look at where the ball actually touches your bat: the front of it, the point facing
            you. A ball carrying backspin is turning such that the front of it is sweeping
            <em>upwards</em> at the moment of contact. The rubber grips that surface, and friction
            pushes back the other way — down. The ball leaves your bat heading for the floor,
            which is why a vertical bat against a backspin serve is a ball in the net rather than
            a ball that's merely a bit low.
          </p>
          <p>
            A topspin ball does the opposite. Its front face is sweeping downwards, so friction
            throws the ball up, and the same vertical bat sends it over the far end line.
          </p>
          <p>
            Opening the face tilts the bounce upwards by enough to cancel the downward drag.
            Closing it tilts the bounce down by enough to cancel the upward kick. The angle you
            need is whatever undoes the spin the other player put on the ball — which is why it
            moves when they change their serve, and why it can't be a habit.
          </p>
          <p class="callout">
            The friction that does all of this is the same friction that decides how a car tyre
            grips. It can only ever push sideways up to a limit set by how hard the two surfaces
            are pressed together — so against a really heavy serve, past a point, no bat angle
            saves you. That's the black region at the edges of the map.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="model" aria-labelledby="model-heading">
        <h2 id="model-heading">What's actually being simulated</h2>
        <div class="prose">
          <p>
            A 2.7 gram, 40 millimetre ball on a regulation 2.74 metre table, stepped through the
            air half a millisecond at a time under gravity, quadratic drag, and Magnus lift taken
            from the cross product of spin and velocity. Contact with the table and contact with
            the bat run through one impulse routine, with the friction impulse capped by the
            Coulomb limit, so the spin the bat imparts obeys the same rule as the spin the table
            scrubs off.
          </p>
          <p>
            The numbers are anchored on measurable things rather than fitted until the picture
            looked right. The Magnus coefficient comes from the observation that a full topspin
            loop — about a hundred revolutions a second at twenty metres a second — pulls down at
            roughly one and three quarter times the ball's own weight.
          </p>
          <p>
            It is deliberately a side view. There is no sidespin, no placement across the width of
            the table, no opponent moving, and the swing speed is fixed so that the two controls
            on this page are the only things that vary. Those are real parts of the game and they
            are all left out, because the claim here is narrow enough to check: at a fixed
            contact point, spin sets the bat angle.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>
    </main>

    <footer>
      <p class="footer-note">
        Built for COMP4020 at the ANU. The physics, the serve presets, and the tests that pin
        them are in
        <a href="https://github.com/comp4020-agentic-coding-studio/comp4020-ass1-wuyimin362-sudo"
          >the source repository</a
        >.
      </p>
    </footer>

    <script>
      import { boot } from "../scripts/app";
      boot();
    </script>
  </body>
</html>
